    add('apple-perfect-pairs','Apple','Count perfect pairs using absolute values',
        'A pair (x, y) is perfect when both conditions hold:\n\n$\\min(|x-y|,|x+y|) \\le \\min(|x|,|y|)$\n\n$\\max(|x-y|,|x+y|) \\ge \\max(|x|,|y|)$.\n\nGiven an integer array arr, count pairs of indices i < j for which (arr[i], arr[j]) is perfect.',None,
        'Put a=|x| and b=|y|. The smaller of |x−y| and |x+y| is |a−b|, and the larger is a+b, so the second condition always holds. The first reduces to max(a,b)≤2×min(a,b). Sort absolute values; for each right endpoint, advance the left pointer until this inequality holds, then add right−left. Two zeros form a perfect pair; zero with a nonzero value does not. O(n log n) time and O(n) space for a sorted copy.',
        section='dsa',topic='two-pointers',type='coding',sources=['IMG-20240824-WA0042.jpg'],
        examples=[{'input':'arr = [2, 5, -3]','output':'2','explanation':'(2, −3) and (5, −3) satisfy both inequalities.'}],notes='The screenshot does not show numeric constraints or a function signature. The example result is derived from the visible definition.')
