    add('meesho-server-index','Meesho','Assign each request to the smallest available server',
        'There are n servers numbered 1…n. Request i arrives at arrival[i] and takes burstTime[i] time. Assign it to the currently available server with the smallest index; report −1 if all servers are busy. A server becomes free exactly at arrival[i]+burstTime[i]. Process requests chronologically, breaking equal-arrival ties by original request index. Return server indices in original request order.',None,
        'Sort requests by (arrival time, original index). Maintain a min-heap of available server IDs and a min-heap of (completion time, server ID) for busy servers. Before assigning a request, release every server whose completion time is ≤ its arrival time. Pop the smallest free ID and schedule its completion, or record −1. O(m log m + (m+n) log n) time and O(m+n) space.',
        section='dsa',topic='heap',type='coding',sources=['IMG-20240731-WA0161.jpg','IMG-20240731-WA0163.jpg','IMG-20240731-WA0165.jpg','WhatsApp Image 2024-08-31 at 12.22.42_e1d512da.jpg'],function_signature='getServerIndex(n, arrival, burstTime)',constraints='1 ≤ n,m ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ arrival[i],burstTime[i] ≤ 10⁹.',
        examples=[{'input':'n = 3\narrival = [2, 4, 1, 8, 9]\nburstTime = [7, 9, 2, 4, 5]','output':'[2, 1, 1, 3, 2]'},{'input':'n = 4\narrival = [3, 5, 1, 6, 8]\nburstTime = [9, 2, 10, 4, 5]','output':'[2, 3, 1, 4, 3]'}],notes='The source calls this round-robin, but its explicit rule always selects the smallest available index; it does not rotate a next-server pointer.')
    add('meesho-move-subsequences','Meesho','Distinct move subsequences on a bounded number line',
        'A number line contains positions 0…n. Each l moves one position left and each r one position right. From a given move string, count distinct subsequence move strings that start at x, stay within the number line, and end at y. Equal resulting move strings are counted once, even if obtained from different character indices.',None,
        'Let dp[p] count distinct valid subsequence strings ending at p, initially dp[x]=1 for the empty string. For a new character c, use a snapshot of dp to compute extensions ending at each valid destination. Subtract the extensions already counted at the previous occurrence of c, stored as a separate vector for each character, then replace that vector with the new extensions. This is distinct-subsequence DP with a position state. O(len(moves)×(n+1)) time and O(n) working space. Apply the specified modulus if available.',
        section='dsa',topic='dp',type='coding',hard=True,sources=['WhatsApp Image 2024-08-31 at 12.22.39_9f3e3c36.jpg'],examples=[{'input':'moves = "rrlrlr", n = 6, x = 1, y = 4','output':'3','explanation':'The three distinct strings are rrr, rrrlr and rrlrr.'}],notes='The screenshot is cropped before the modulus value and numeric constraints. Distinct-string counting is inferred from the three strings in its example. The empty-string rule when x=y is not shown; clarify it before implementing a judge.')
    add('de-shaw-normalize','DE Shaw','Normalize item costs over m days',
        'Given n positive integer costs and m days, each day you may decrease one currently maximum-cost item by 1 and increase one currently minimum-cost item by 1. Ties affect only one chosen item per operation. Find the minimum attainable difference between maximum and minimum cost after m days.',None,
        'Avoid simulating up to 10⁹ days. Sort costs and use prefix sums to compute the work to raise all values below a lower threshold, and to lower all values above an upper threshold. Binary-search the highest lower threshold achievable with m unit increases and the lowest upper threshold achievable with m unit decreases. Before the two fronts meet, their difference is the answer. Once they meet, the conserved total permits range 0 if divisible by n, otherwise range 1. Handle n=1 separately. O(n log n+n log V) time with a linear threshold check, O(n) space; prefix-sum/bisect checks can reduce the threshold-check cost.',
        section='dsa',topic='binary-search',type='coding',hard=True,sources=['IMG-20240728-WA0005.jpg','IMG-20240728-WA0006.jpg','IMG-20240728-WA0007.jpg','IMG-20240728-WA0008.jpg'],function_signature='getMinDiff(cost, m)',constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ cost[i],m ≤ 10⁹.',
        examples=[{'input':'cost = [1, 1, 4, 2], m = 1','output':'2','explanation':'One day can produce [2, 1, 3, 2].'}],notes='The visible example applies both the maximum decrease and minimum increase in one day. The explanation follows that paired-transfer interpretation.')
